","support_assessment":"supports_bounded_attribution"}]},{"id":"claim:healthgeo05:mckittrick_struggle","subject":"work:mckittrick_demonic_2006","predicate":"assesses_scope","object":"concept:healthgeo05:black_geographies","statement":"McKittrick treats racial and sexual domination as spatial processes while preserving Black women’s geographic knowledge and contestation.","qualification":"Chapter 5 pp. 121–123 only, read in the authorized 2020 reprint. Not a new 2020 programme or a whole-book reading.","intervention_year":2006,"attributed_to":"work:mckittrick_demonic_2006","basis":"editorial_interpretation","valid_time":null,"review":{"status":"accepted","reviewed_on":"2026-09-18","reviewer":"Codex editorial integration review","rationale":"Original pp. 121–122 describe domination and spatial contestation together. The 2020 reprint preserves a 2006 intervention; a new 2026 edition is outside the reading scope."},"history":[{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"proposed","reason":"User-prioritized coverage and identity review"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"recommended_for_bounded_release","decision_record":"data/extension-2026/release-candidate-06/review-decisions.json"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"accepted_into_production","revision":"1.122","decision_record":"data/production-batches/extension-1.122/acceptance.json"}],"evidence":[{"source_record_id":"source:healthgeo05:mckittrick_demonic_2006","locator":"Chapter 5, original printed pp. 121–123, in 2020 authorized reprint; PDF pp. 4–6","check_status":"passage_checked","scope":"passage","support":"McKittrick treats racial and sexual domination as spatial processes while preserving Black women’s geographic knowledge and contestation.","limitation":"Chapter 5 pp. 121–123 only, read in the authorized 2020 reprint. Not a new 2020 programme or a whole-book reading.","checked_on":"2026-09-18","support_assessment":"supports_bounded_attribution"}]},{"id":"claim:healthgeo05:mckittrick_wynter","subject":"work:mckittrick_demonic_2006","predicate":"assesses_scope","object":"concept:healthgeo05:geography","statement":"McKittrick explicitly develops a geographic reading of Sylvia Wynter’s critique of the overrepresentation of Man.","qualification":"Chapter 5 pp. 122–123; bounded account of her stated use of Wynter. No generalized genealogy or claim of first reception.","intervention_year":2006,"attributed_to":"work:mckittrick_demonic_2006","basis":"editorial_interpretation","valid_time":null,"review":{"status":"accepted","reviewed_on":"2026-09-18","reviewer":"Codex editorial integration review","rationale":"Original pp. 122–123 explicitly explains the geographic use of Sylvia Wynter. Exact conceptual target retained; no author-to-author influence edge invented."},"history":[{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"proposed","reason":"User-prioritized coverage and identity review"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"recommended_for_bounded_release","decision_record":"data/extension-2026/release-candidate-06/review-decisions.json"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"accepted_into_production","revision":"1.122","decision_record":"data/production-batches/extension-1.122/acceptance.json"}],"evidence":[{"source_record_id":"source:healthgeo05:mckittrick_demonic_2006","locator":"Original printed pp. 122–123, reprint PDF pp. 5–6; lines 131–187","check_status":"passage_checked","scope":"passage","support":"McKittrick explicitly develops a geographic reading of Sylvia Wynter’s critique of the overrepresentation of Man.","limitation":"Chapter 5 pp. 122–123; bounded account of her stated use of Wynter. No generalized genealogy or claim of first reception.","checked_on":"2026-09-18","support_assessment":"supports_bounded_attribution"}]},{"id":"claim:healthgeo05:gregory_healey_methods","subject":"work:gregory_healey_hgis_2007","predicate":"assesses_scope","object":"concept:healthgeo05:geography","statement":"Gregory and Healey describe historical GIS as extending from quantitative into qualitative inquiry and across disciplinary boundaries.","qualification":"Author abstract only; GIS is one method within historical geography, not its beginning or its synonym.","intervention_year":2007,"attributed_to":"work:gregory_healey_hgis_2007","basis":"editorial_interpretation","valid_time":null,"review":{"status":"accepted","reviewed_on":"2026-09-18","reviewer":"Codex editorial integration review","rationale":"The author-deposited abstract explicitly states quantitative origins, qualitative extension and cross-disciplinary reach. Accept that bounded self-description, not the unseen evaluation or a claim that GIS defines geography."},"history":[{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"proposed","reason":"User-prioritized coverage and identity review"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"recommended_for_bounded_release","decision_record":"data/extension-2026/release-candidate-06/review-decisions.json"},{"date":"2026-09-18","action":"accepted_into_production","revision":"1.122","decision_record":"data/production-batches/extension-1.122/acceptance.json"}],"evidence":[{"source_record_id":"source:healthgeo05:gregory_healey_hgis_2007","locator":"Author-deposited abstract; repository explicitly has no full text","check_status":"abstract_checked","scope":"abstract","support":"Gregory and Healey describe historical GIS as extending from quantitative into qualitative inquiry and across disciplinary boundaries.","limitation":"Author abstract only; GIS is one method within historical geography, not its beginning or its synonym.","checked_on":"2026-09-18","support_assessment":"supports_bounded_attribution"}]},{"id":"claim:healthgeo05:machado_gomes_traditions","subject":"work:machado_gomes_geografia_2013","predicate":"assesses_scope","object":"concept:healthgeo05:geography","statement":"Machado and Gomes distinguish Abreu’s urban-historical work from Moraes’s inquiries into geopolitics and territorial management in Brazilian historical geography.","qualification":"Portuguese abstract read; English research paraphrase. Their two 